<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santiago</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1541">1541</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Santiago de Chile</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santiago is the capital and largest city of Chile, in the central valley at the foot of the Andes. It was founded on 12 February 1541 by the Spanish conquistador <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-valdivia">Pedro de Valdivia</a> and became the seat of the governors and, from 1609, of the royal audiencia of Chile. It has been the capital of the independent republic since independence in 1818.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santiago.jpg" alt="Santiago" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miguel hernandez · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palacio_de_La_Moneda_-_miguelreflex.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The conquests of the thirteenth century transferred vast estates in Andalusia to the crown, the nobility, the Church and the military orders of <b>Santiago</b>, Calatrava and Alcántara, laying the foundations of a powerful landed aristocracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Christian settlers were drawn to the frontier by generous charters such as the fuero of Jaca, which became a model for towns along the pilgrim road to <b>Santiago</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…the eleventh century, when tribute in gold (parias) from the taifa kingdoms of al-Andalus enriched the court and the Church, and the pilgrimage road to the shrine of Saint James at <b>Santiago</b> brought French pilgrims, merchants and settlers, with communities of Franks growing up at Sahagún and León.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>By favouring the pilgrimage route to <b>Santiago</b> and its Frankish settlers, Navarre helped tie northern Spain to the Romanesque culture and church reform of France; the route, the Camino Francés, remains one of the most travelled pilgrim roads in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The early kingdom was made by war against al-Andalus, fought by royal hosts, town militias and the military orders of the Templars, Hospitallers, <b>Santiago</b> and Avis, and later the Order of Christ, which Denis founded in 1319 to take over the Templars’ property.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Santiago</b> a governing junta formed on 18 September 1810, a date still celebrated as the national holiday, opening the period known as the Patria Vieja, in which creole leaders such as José Miguel Carrera and Bernardo O'Higgins quarrelled while Viceroy José Fernando de Abascal sent royalist forces…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
